                "xpath": "/html[1]/body[1]/div[4]/div[2]/div[1]/div[12]/div[3]/div[1]/div[1]/div[1]/div[1]",
                "asynchronous_ai_overview": false,
                "markdown": "![Hebrew letter Qof Stock Vector Image &amp; Art - Alamy](https://api.dataforseo.com/cdn/i/10110525-2762-0139-0000-4fb643715612:4)\nThe Hebrew letter **Qof** (also spelled **Kuf** or **Khof** ) is the `19th letter of the Hebrew alphabet` (`ק` ) and carries the numerical value of **100** in Gematria.[[1]](https://www.hebrew4christians.com/Grammar/Unit_One/Aleph-Bet/Qof/qof.html)[[2]](https://www.equipgodspeople.com/bible-study-tools/lexicons-word-study/old-testament-hebrew/qof/)\n\n### Key Facts & Meaning\n\n- **Pronunciation:** Historically pronounced as a deep, emphatic uvular stop (`[q]` ), it is pronounced as a standard `k` sound in Modern Hebrew (merging phonetically with the letter *Kaf*).[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qoph)[[2]](https://www.hebrew4christians.com/Grammar/Unit_One/Aleph-Bet/Qof/qof.html)\n- **Visual Structure:** It is unique because its left leg descends below the baseline of the line of text, which is visually formed by combining a *Resh* on top with a descending stroke (`Zayin` ) below.[[1]](https://gabrielelevy.com/it/pages/the-letter-qof)[[2]](https://learnhebrewforchristians.com/hebrew-alphabet/qof)\n- **Literal Meanings:** Ancient pictographs and traditions link Qof to the back of the head, the eye of a needle, or a monkey.[[1]](https://gabrielelevy.com/pages/the-letter-qof)[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qoph)\n- **Symbolism:** In Jewish mysticism (such as insights shared via [Chabad.org](https://www.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/137091/jewish/Kuf.htm) ), Qof represents a dual nature—symbolizing both the sacred (*Kedushah* ) and the superficial or profane husk (*Klipah* ), requiring a person to look deeper past the surface.[[1]](https://learnhebrewforchristians.com/hebrew-alphabet/qof)[[2]](https://gabrielelevy.com/pages/the-letter-qof)[[3]](https://gabrielelevy.com/it/pages/the-letter-qof)\n- **Common Hebrew Words:** \n\t- `קָדוֹשׁ` (*Kadosh* - holy or set apart)\n\t- `קוֹל` (*Kol* - voice)\n\t- `קָרָא` (*Kara* - to call or read)[[1]](https://learnhebrewforchristians.com/hebrew-alphabet/qof)\n\nWould you like to explore **how Qof is used in Bible verses (like Psalm 119)** , or learn how to **write it in Hebrew cursive script**?",
